// Historical copy specification for this one-time refinement. Canonical edits live in workspace/index.html.
const lessons = {};
function add(id,title,purpose,sections,reading){lessons[id]={title,purpose,sections,reading};}
add('ce1-01','What counts as evidence of a strength?',
'A career idea becomes more useful when you can explain what led you to it. You will learn to distinguish what someone enjoys, what they have actually done and what they want work to support before reading the experience notebook.',[
['Separate interest, skill and value',[
'An <strong>interest</strong> is something that attracts your attention or that you enjoy. A <strong>skill</strong> is an ability used to carry out a task. A <strong>work value</strong> is something important to you about work, such as variety, helping people or predictable hours. Enjoying an activity does not establish how well you can do it; completing it successfully does not establish that you want to do it every day.',
'A <strong>career goal</strong> describes a direction for work or learning. A <strong>life goal</strong> describes something you want your wider life to support. Both matter when exploring a role. A role can use a skill while conflicting with a schedule or other priority.']],
['Build the claim from the action',[
'A <strong>claim</strong> is a statement you want a reader to accept. <strong>Evidence</strong> is the information supporting it. Look for the situation, a specific action and an observed result. In a simple example, a learner rewrites confusing club directions and two readers then follow them without help. This supports some skill in explaining information. It does not establish that the learner can teach every subject.',
'Use the sequence <strong>recorded action → possible skill → limit → question about work</strong>. Keep the person’s contribution separate from a team’s result. A transferable skill may be useful in another setting, while technical training may still be needed.']]
], 'When you reach the notebook, first find something the person actually did. Keep their reflection about enjoyment and preferred hours separate from the action evidence. You will practise turning those different clues into a cautious direction to investigate.');
add('ce1-02','Know what an occupation profile can tell you',
'Career websites contain several kinds of information. Before reading the profile and employer notice, learn which question each kind can answer. This will help you compare work with a person’s interests and preferred conditions.',[
['Distinguish a job, an occupation and a career',[
'A <strong>job</strong> is a particular position with an employer. An <strong>occupation</strong> groups work with similar duties. A <strong>career</strong> is a longer path that may include several jobs, learning and other life roles. An <strong>occupation profile</strong> describes a kind of work across settings. A <strong>job posting</strong> advertises a particular opening.',
'A National Occupational Classification, or <strong>NOC</strong>, code identifies an occupational group used in Canadian information. It helps you check whether sources cover the same group. It is not a ranking of people or a guarantee that every job in that group has the same conditions.']],
['Sort a statement before using it',[
'A <strong>duty</strong> says what someone does. A <strong>working condition</strong> says when, where or under what demands. <strong>Preparation</strong> describes education, experience or other evidence used to enter the work. In a simple notice, “prepare notices” is a duty, “evening shifts” is a condition and “bring examples of your writing” describes preparation.',
'Read in three passes: find the relevant field, explain its fact in your own words, then state what it supports and leaves open. Keep the publisher, place and dates attached. “Common” and “preferred” describe different strengths of statement from “required.” An access date tells you when a page was read; it does not make an older section newly updated.']]
], 'Use the profile for broad information and the posting for that employer’s opening. Before the first reading check, identify whether the sentence describes work, working conditions, preparation or the source itself.');
add('ce1-03','How to compare two routes toward a role',
'A route is the learning and work steps toward a possible role. Comparing routes means checking whether each can lead toward the stated destination and fit the learner’s preparation, time and money.',[
['Read entry and completion separately',[
'A <strong>prerequisite</strong> is something that must be completed before a later step. A <strong>credential</strong> is a formal record of a qualification, such as a certificate. Entry requirements tell you what is needed to begin; the completion record tells you what the route awards. Neither automatically guarantees a job.',
'First read the employer’s role notice to see which routes it considers. Then read each training notice. A route with the right-sounding title cannot be assumed to qualify someone for an opening. Meeting academic prerequisites also does not remove a separately stated selection process.']],
['Compare using the same questions',[
'A <strong>criterion</strong> is a question used to judge an option: can the learner enter, finish by the deadline, meet the full listed cost and develop the relevant skills? Apply the same criteria to both routes. Add one-time fees and supplies, then multiply a monthly cost by the number of months it applies.',
'For a small invented example, $100 in fees plus $20 monthly travel for three months costs $160 in those categories. The $100 headline alone omits $60. Check what the total includes before comparing it with the available budget.',
'A <strong>trade-off</strong> is a benefit given up or a cost accepted when choosing. Use this decision path: define the destination → check entry → compare time, costs and learning → choose provisionally → state what would make you reconsider. A backup must have a workable entry path; simply naming a second program does not make it ready to use.']]
], 'Read the role notice before the route notices. The first check asks why the routes can be compared for this particular role. Later you will calculate each complete listed cost and distinguish a route available now from a backup needing more preparation.');
add('ce1-05','How courses, prerequisites and deadlines form a route',
'A high-school route connects what a learner has completed with what a future program requires. A useful plan shows the order of preparation, the dates it must fit and the time and money available.',[
['Read the condition before scheduling the course',[
'<strong>Required</strong> means a stated condition must be met. <strong>Recommended</strong> means it may help but is not stated as compulsory. A <strong>prerequisite chain</strong> is an ordered sequence: if Course A is needed for Course B, and B for C, the plan must put A before B before C.',
'A <strong>portfolio</strong> is a selected collection of work showing what someone can do. A provider may require portfolio pieces without requiring one particular school course to produce them. Keep the provider’s requirement separate from the school’s available preparation route.']],
['Check backwards and forwards',[
'Work backwards from the deadline to identify what must be ready. Work forwards from completed preparation to find which next steps are possible. Then check when the school offers them. In a simple example, a sample due in May cannot depend on a course that finishes in June.',
'<strong>Capacity</strong> is the available time, course space or other resource for the plan. Count travel, additional work and materials when supplied. Shared preparation can support two directions, but one slot cannot hold two courses at once.',
'A <strong>checkpoint</strong> is a planned review moment; a <strong>trigger</strong> is a change that requires another review. Build the plan as destination → requirements → prerequisite order → dates and resource limits → checkpoints.']]
], 'When you read the program notices, separate required items from recommendations. Use the school timetable to decide when the preparation is actually possible. The first reading check concerns that distinction, before you build the full plan.');
add('ce1-06','Different ways of working carry different responsibilities',
'An interest can lead toward employment or a small venture. Before comparing the offers, learn who arranges the work, which costs each person carries and what the stated money amounts represent.',[
['Separate employment from running a venture',[
'An <strong>employee</strong> works under an employer’s arrangements and direction. A <strong>self-employed</strong> person obtains work from clients and takes responsibility for providing the service. <strong>Entrepreneurship</strong> involves developing an opportunity to offer a product or service and accepting uncertainty about demand and costs.',
'The same task can appear in either arrangement. The important difference may be who finds customers, sets the schedule, buys materials, provides training and handles problems. A small venture still has responsibilities.']],
['Read the money and evidence honestly',[
'<strong>Revenue</strong> is sales money before expenses. An <strong>expense</strong> is a cost of providing the service. For an invented example, five sales at $10 bring $50 revenue. Subtracting $15 of listed materials leaves $35 before any unlisted costs or time. Calling that complete take-home pay would hide missing information.',
'An employee’s <strong>gross wage</strong> is earnings before payroll deductions. A venture’s remainder after some costs is a different measure. Compare the categories and limits before comparing the amounts.',
'Use this path: identify the arrangement → assign responsibilities → label earnings and costs → check training, time and uncertain demand → propose a suitable learning step. School or community experience can support an application without being renamed as paid employment.']]
], 'Read the shop offer for what the employer supplies, then the venture record for what the operator must arrange. The first question asks who carries one stated responsibility. Use the actual offer rather than assumptions about all businesses.');
add('ce1-07','How to revise a plan without losing its purpose',
'A change in time or responsibilities can make an old plan stop fitting. Revising means checking what changed and finding a workable next step toward the goal.',[
['Separate the goal, route and timing',[
'A <strong>goal</strong> is what you want to accomplish. A <strong>route</strong> is how you work toward it. <strong>Timing</strong> places it into an actual day or week. A new commitment may block a time slot while leaving the goal useful. New evidence may also change the goal itself; identify which part needs revision.',
'A <strong>constraint</strong> is a limit the plan must respect. <strong>Capacity</strong> is the time or resources actually available. A <strong>priority</strong> is something that needs attention before another task. Do not assume every listed commitment can move.']],
['Count a complete block of time',[
'Include preparation, travel, the activity and return or transition time. For a simple example, a 45-minute activity with 15 minutes of travel each way needs 75 minutes. A one-hour free block is too short even though the activity itself is less than an hour.',
'A <strong>buffer</strong> is unassigned time that allows for variation. Protect the rest and essential commitments given in the case. Digital reminders can help, while repeated notifications can use the task’s reserved time; choose a habit that supports the plan.',
'Use old assumption → new evidence → revised route or time → next review. A review trigger names what would require another look, such as a changed timetable.']]
], 'Read the original plan and the changed weekly schedule together. Before judging a route, check its whole time block against fixed commitments. The first question asks what changed that prevents the old arrangement from fitting.');
module.exports = lessons;
add('co1-01','What makes an application statement believable?',
'A first application can use school or community experience. The task is to show a relevant action truthfully, so an employer can see why it relates to the work being advertised.',[
['Connect the employer’s task with the applicant’s action',[
'A <strong>job posting</strong> is an employer’s advertisement for an opening. A <strong>duty</strong> is work the person would do. An <strong>experience record</strong> describes what the applicant has already done. These documents have different jobs: one states what is needed; the other supplies possible evidence.',
'A <strong>quality claim</strong> names a characteristic, such as “careful” or “helpful.” An <strong>observable action</strong> tells a reader what happened, such as “checked the names against the list.” A claim becomes more credible when the action fits it.']],
['Build a short context–action–result statement',[
'<strong>Context</strong> explains where and why the action mattered. <strong>Action</strong> names what the person did. <strong>Result</strong> states what happened afterwards. For a simple example: “During a club event, I checked the room number against the booking sheet and corrected the notice. Visitors reached the booked room.” This could support a checking duty; it does not establish professional management experience.',
'Match a posting requirement → find a record entry showing a relevant action → explain the context, action and supplied result → remove anything the record does not establish. An applicant can contribute to a team result without claiming to have done everyone’s work.']]
], 'Next you will meet Rowan, read the employer’s notice and inspect Rowan’s preparation notes. Look first for the work the employer actually needs; then select an action that shows Rowan doing something relevant. The later sections demonstrate and support the writing.');
add('co1-02','Know the difference between a task and permission to do it',
'A first-shift sheet explains the work and its limits. Before using it to make decisions, distinguish what a helper is responsible for, what they are allowed to do and what they need to learn.',[
['Read responsibility, authorization and training separately',[
'A <strong>responsibility</strong> is something a role is expected to do. <strong>Authorization</strong> is permission for a particular action. <strong>Training</strong> is instruction and practice needed to carry it out. Being asked to help at a desk does not automatically authorize changing a reservation or using unfamiliar equipment.',
'A <strong>boundary</strong> identifies what must stay protected, such as accurate records, private participant information or people’s safety. A task can be familiar while a particular action still needs approval.']],
['Choose a permitted step and a clear message',[
'First identify the situation and relevant boundary. If information or permission is missing, pause that action. Use a safe, permitted task while waiting only when doing so does not leave an immediate hazard unattended. A floor plan can help locate a clear route and the responsible person.',
'For a simple example, an unready login is a reason to leave the record unchanged and ask for the approved access process. “The login is not ready; I have left the record unchanged. Can you show me the approved process?” identifies the problem, action already taken and needed response.',
'Use notice → boundary → safe permitted action → specific message → required instruction or approval. The supplied brief determines who may authorize work in these cases.']]
], 'Read the shift brief and floor plan for permission labels, privacy rules, safe routes and contact information. The first check asks which task is already permitted. The later cases ask you to use the same boundaries when something is uncertain or unsafe.');
add('co1-03','How to answer an interview question with evidence',
'An interview explores how a person’s preparation fits a role. This lesson uses written rehearsal, so you can learn to explain an experience clearly without a live interview or recording.',[
['Choose an experience that answers the question',[
'A <strong>behavioural question</strong> asks how someone acted in a situation, for example, “Tell me about a time a plan changed.” Identify the skill being explored, then choose a record containing that kind of event. An unrelated achievement does not explain how the person handled change.',
'Give the situation and responsibility, describe the person’s own actions, and finish with the supplied result. You can add learning where it helps. The structure is a guide to meaning; you do not have to say its labels in the answer.']],
['Own the action and share the result accurately',[
'A <strong>contribution</strong> is the person’s part in the work. A <strong>team result</strong> belongs to everyone who helped. In a simple example, “I checked the supply list; another helper packed the boxes; together we finished before opening” explains both. “I completed everything alone” would change the evidence.',
'A <strong>limit</strong> marks what the example cannot prove. One event does not establish years of professional experience. Rehearse by reading as if the listener knows nothing about the event: can they identify the problem, personal action and result?',
'Use question → relevant experience → personal responsibility and action → result → learning or next question. A focused employer question can ask about training or duties without claiming a conversation has already occurred.']]
], 'Read the role brief and experience records to distinguish Rowan’s own action from what another helper or the whole team did. The first check asks which description matches Rowan’s contribution. Later you will turn that evidence into an interview answer.');
add('co1-04','How to change a work plan when something blocks it',
'A changed shift needs an updated order of actions. A deadline matters, but permission, training, safety and privacy still govern the response.',[
['Identify blockers and dependencies',[
'A <strong>blocker</strong> prevents a task from progressing as planned. An <strong>alternative</strong> is another way to achieve the purpose. A <strong>dependency</strong> is a step that must happen before another can begin. If a supervisor must approve a location, approval comes before moving the activity there.',
'A <strong>priority</strong> depends on consequences and order. Immediate safety and privacy needs take attention before routine tasks. <strong>Parallel</strong> work happens at the same time, but it requires available people or resources; one person cannot perform two separate hands-on tasks simultaneously.']],
['Report early and check the remaining time',[
'A useful update states the time, blocker, affected deadline and request. For a simple example: “The copier stopped at 9:00; notices are needed by 10:00. May I prepare the approved blank version?” This lets the responsible person decide before the delay grows.',
'Work backwards from the deadline. A 15-minute task due at 10:00 must start by 9:45. Then check whether permission and earlier steps can finish in time. Doing unapproved work quickly does not make the plan valid.',
'Use protect people and information → report → identify approval and dependencies → assign workable tasks and times → check the deadline. A successful finish can still leave a process improvement worth recording.']]
], 'Read the supplied shift rules before judging the incident. Locate who approves alternatives and which steps must precede setup. The first reading check asks about that authority; the later tasks add time and changing conditions.');
add('co1-05','How to turn feedback into a useful next step',
'Feedback helps a learner revise a plan when it identifies something that happened and what could be improved. Learn to separate evidence from a broad judgement before reading the feedback records.',[
['Separate observation, interpretation and growth area',[
'An <strong>observation</strong> describes what someone noticed. An <strong>interpretation</strong> explains what it may suggest. A <strong>growth area</strong> names a specific behaviour or ability needing practice. “The learner checked six entries accurately” is an observation. “This suggests careful checking” is a limited interpretation. “The learner is perfect at every records task” goes beyond that evidence.',
'The same distinction applies to a difficulty. A delayed report can suggest practising earlier updates; it does not establish that the person is generally unreliable. A record of simulated practice should remain labelled as that kind of learning.']],
['Make the next test observable',[
'A <strong>growth action</strong> says what the person will do differently. A <strong>criterion</strong> says how to check it. “Improve communication” is broad; “include the blocker, deadline and request in a two-sentence update” gives something to examine in the next supplied scenario.',
'An employment arrangement also needs reading: part-time concerns hours, temporary work has an expected endpoint, and seasonal work occurs during a part of the year. Those labels alone do not establish exact hours, benefits or fit. Add the whole schedule, including other commitments.',
'Use observation → cautious skill claim → growth action → next test and criterion → direction that fits the available time and resources.']]
], 'Find the feedback comment containing an actual observation before choosing a strength claim. Later, compare the supplied work arrangements with the stated weekly limits and propose a growth test using the course scenarios.');
add('co2-01','How to check an employment rule against a shift',
'A rule can apply differently depending on a worker’s age, employment arrangement, tasks and hours. This lesson teaches how to connect the supplied facts with the relevant row in an official-rule record.',[
['Know what the documents establish',[
'An <strong>employee</strong> works for an employer. An <strong>employment standard</strong> is a minimum employment rule in the applicable system. <strong>Jurisdiction</strong> identifies which rules or authority apply. For these cases, read the supplied Alberta youth-employment record rather than applying a general idea about all teen work.',
'A <strong>permit</strong> is official permission for specified employment. Parent or guardian consent is a different condition. <strong>Adult supervision</strong> means an adult supervises the work as described in the record; another worker’s experience or an adult’s reachable telephone number does not establish that condition.']],
['Match the facts before deciding',[
'Use age → employment arrangement → workplace and task → day and time to locate the relevant rule. Separate a shift where supervision or the calendar day changes. One condition being met does not cancel another condition: for example, permission cannot be assumed to remove a stated hours restriction.',
'A <strong>mismatch</strong> is a supplied fact conflicting with a rule. An <strong>unknown</strong> is a relevant fact not supplied. State a mismatch with the matching rule; ask a specific question for an unknown. Keep personal constraints, such as transport, distinct from rules applying to a group of workers.',
'For a simple reading example, a rule requiring adult coverage until closing needs evidence about who covers the final portion of the shift. “An adult supervises the opening” leaves a different question unanswered.']]
], 'Read the worker record, draft offer and dated rule record together. Locate the supervisor’s age and coverage times before the first check. All rules and facts needed here are supplied; the task is a fictional source-based review rather than a real employment determination.');
add('co2-02','Recognize a hazard before choosing a response',
'A workplace request does not supply the knowledge needed to carry it out safely. Before reading the hazard case, learn to separate an observed condition, a possible harm and the information needed to respond.',[
['Describe a hazard without creating exposure',[
'A <strong>hazard</strong> is something with the potential to cause harm. <strong>Risk</strong> concerns the possibility and seriousness of that harm in the circumstances. A <strong>control</strong> is a measure used to prevent or reduce exposure. An unreadable product label is an observable information gap; it does not identify the substance.',
'Observation does not require touching, testing or deliberately smelling something unsafe. “The label cannot be read” reports a fact. “Handling it without instructions could cause harm” states the concern. A claim about its exact chemical identity would need evidence the observer may not have.']],
['Choose the immediate safe action first',[
'Use identify → pause and avoid guessing → report and request information or training → reassess using the responsible assessment and required controls. The first response can stop exposure while a competent person decides the final control. Do not select protective equipment for an unknown hazard by guessing.',
'The <strong>right to know</strong> connects to hazard information and instruction; the <strong>right to participate</strong> connects to reporting and safety assessment. Dangerous-work refusal has an applicable threshold and process; the dated Alberta record in this lesson explains them. A concern should be communicated through that process rather than treated as permission to resume on appearance alone.',
'For a simple example, unfamiliar equipment with a missing instruction is a reason to stay clear, report the gap and ask for approved training—not to experiment to prove whether it is dangerous.']]
], 'Read the observation, training information and official-process record before deciding what the worker can do. The first check asks which sentence reports the evidence accurately. The four-step image and the later teaching will connect that observation to a response.');
add('co2-03','What a useful concern report needs to establish',
'A concern report should help someone who was not present understand the event, the response already taken and what remains unresolved. Learn to keep those parts separate before reading the timeline.',[
['Separate a report from a confirmed resolution',[
'<strong>Documentation</strong> is a record preserving relevant facts and actions. An <strong>observation</strong> states what was seen or heard. A <strong>request</strong> states what response is needed. An <strong>acknowledgement</strong> confirms that a message was received; it does not establish that a hazard was corrected or a payment fixed.',
'For a simple example, “The worker reported the missing guard at 10:00; the supervisor received the message at 10:10” records a report and receipt. It does not confirm inspection, repair or permission to use the equipment. Record those outcomes only when evidence supplies them.']],
['Make the evidence and route traceable',[
'Use who, date and time, location or record, observed fact, action already taken and requested response. Distinguish an exact supplied quotation from a summary and your observation from another person’s statement. Avoid guessing a person’s intention.',
'A pay concern needs the same pay period and kind of amount on both records. Gross regular earnings are hours multiplied by the supplied rate; net pay is after deductions. A different net deposit alone does not establish a missing regular-hours payment.',
'Choose a route suited to the concern: workplace safety, wages and protected-ground treatment involve different responsibilities and official processes. The supplied source records explain the relevant steps. Preserve original records and add dated corrections rather than changing originals to make them agree.']]
], 'Read the chronology for what is confirmed at each time. The first check asks about the status of the concern at a particular point. Keep “reported,” “received,” “being checked” and “resolved” distinct while writing your later response.');
add('co2-04','Recognize a protected-ground concern using evidence',
'Unfair or upsetting treatment needs careful examination. This lesson teaches how to document a possible discrimination concern and identify a suitable review route using supplied fictional records.',[
['Connect the event, effect and protected ground',[
'A <strong>protected ground</strong> is a characteristic protected by the applicable human-rights law; the source record lists the relevant grounds for this lesson. A discrimination concern involves a negative effect connected to a protected ground. A disappointing result alone does not establish that connection.',
'Look for the employment event, what changed for the worker and the evidence connecting the effect to the ground. An <strong>inference</strong> is an interpretation drawn from facts. Keep it separate from the facts themselves. An email explaining a decision can be relevant evidence; a guess about someone’s private motive is different.']],
['Understand accommodation and a review request',[
'<strong>Accommodation</strong> is a process to address a barrier connected to a protected need. The actual work requirement, relevant need and reasonable options matter. It does not automatically award a preferred shift or remove the job’s essential work.',
'In a simple example, a learner may need written confirmation of instructions to perform a task accurately. The question is how the communication barrier can be addressed while keeping accurate work—not whether a label alone proves inability.',
'Use event → negative effect → possible protected-ground connection → relevant accommodation question → suitable support route. Preserve dates and supplied words, limit private information, and request an individual review rather than guessing intent. The dated source explains process and time limits; no real complaint or confrontation is required here.']]
], 'When you read the timeline, identify which words or decisions connect the changed work opportunity to the protected ground. The first check asks for that evidence. Later you will distinguish a supported concern from information still needed for a fair review.');
add('fl1-01','Understand the parts of a pay stub',
'A pay stub is an employer’s written breakdown of a payment for work. Before reading one, learn which work period it covers and how earnings become the amount paid.',[
['Follow gross pay, deductions and net pay',[
'<strong>Gross pay</strong> is earnings before deductions. For regular hourly work, use the supplied hours × hourly rate. A <strong>deduction</strong> is an amount removed from gross pay. <strong>Net pay</strong> is the amount remaining after the listed deductions.',
'In a small invented example, three hours at $18 produces $54 gross earnings. If the statement lists $6 of deductions, net pay is $48. These are supplied practice amounts, not tax or contribution rates. Deductions have already been removed from net pay; subtracting them again would count them twice.']],
['Match the period before comparing records',[
'A <strong>pay period</strong> identifies the work included. The <strong>payment date</strong> says when the money is paid. <strong>Current</strong> means this period; <strong>year to date</strong> combines payments in the calendar year. Current gross and year-to-date deductions belong to different comparisons.',
'Use matching time entries → current hours and rate → current gross → current deductions → current net → deposit. A deposit matching the net box does not prove the hours are correct. Read the pay-period dates on the stub and time record before checking the amounts.',
'If two records disagree, state the specific line and difference. The mismatch does not by itself tell you whether the cause was recording, approval or processing.']]
], 'Begin by locating the pay period and the current-hours line. The first reading check asks which records to compare to check the hours for this payment. Later you will calculate the supplied gross, deductions and net.');
add('fl1-02','Work out what is available before weighing a purchase',
'A money decision has an amount, a purpose and influences around it. Before reading the purchase situation, learn to protect existing commitments and recognize what may be pushing the choice.',[
['Separate money held from money available',[
'A <strong>commitment</strong> is money already needed for a planned obligation. A <strong>constraint</strong> is a limit the choice must respect. The money visible in an account is not necessarily all available for a new purchase.',
'For a simple example, someone has $50, with $20 needed for travel and $10 for supplies. Available for a new choice is $50 − $20 − $10 = $20. A $30 item can be paid for physically, but buying it would leave too little for those commitments.',
'A <strong>need</strong> is a requirement in the situation; a <strong>want</strong> is a desire without the same immediate necessity. Identify the function needed. A usable bag and a particular brand of bag do not necessarily serve different essential functions.']],
['Recognize influences without letting them do the arithmetic',[
'A <strong>contextual influence</strong> comes from circumstances or relationships, such as transport options or group plans. A <strong>psychological influence</strong> comes from feelings, thoughts or habits. A <strong>marketing influence</strong> comes from how an offer is presented, such as a deadline or highlighted price.',
'These can overlap. Wanting to belong and seeing a limited-time offer can make a purchase feel urgent; they do not increase the available amount. Use commitments → money left → benefit sought → pressure or assumption → feasible choice and consequence. A choice should explain what is protected and what is postponed.']]
], 'Read the money note for the existing commitments before interpreting the advertisement. The first check asks what remains after those amounts. Later you will explain the influences and a choice that fits the supplied situation.');
add('fl1-03','A budget must fit both the total and the calendar',
'A monthly total can look affordable while a bill arrives before the money needed to pay it. This lesson teaches two checks: the whole-period plan and the money available on each date.',[
['Learn the budget and cash-flow terms',[
'A <strong>budget</strong> plans income, spending and money set aside. A <strong>cash-flow calendar</strong> puts money received and paid in date order. The <strong>opening balance</strong> is the money available at the start; a <strong>running balance</strong> is the amount after each movement.',
'Income minus expenses and planned saving gives a <strong>surplus</strong> if positive or a <strong>shortfall</strong> if negative. That whole-period result does not establish that every bill can be paid on time. Money due later is not available earlier.']],
['Carry the balance through the dates',[
'For a small invented example, start with $30. Pay a $20 bill on Wednesday: $10 remains. Receive $40 on Friday: $50 is then available. The Friday payment does not change what was available on Wednesday.',
'Use opening balance + income received − payments made = balance at this point. Carry that result into the next date. Identify the lowest balance, as well as the final one. In the lesson’s model, a negative balance signals using money not available; no overdraft terms are supplied.',
'A <strong>buffer</strong> is accessible money reserved for variation. A changed payment date works only when the case confirms the arrangement. Removing a necessary expense or assuming an unapproved extension hides the problem rather than establishing a workable plan.']]
], 'Read the opening money, income dates and bill dates together. The first reading check concerns money available on one date. Later you will test the full total and calendar and explain a confirmed adjustment.');
add('fl1-04','Compare an agreement’s total and its first payment',
'A major purchase can be paid for in several ways. A small monthly figure does not tell you the total cost, when money is needed or what ownership and protection the agreement supplies.',[
['Read the payment and ownership terms',[
'An <strong>upfront cost</strong> is paid at the beginning. An <strong>instalment</strong> is one payment in a series. A <strong>recurring cost</strong> repeats. The agreement’s <strong>term</strong> describes its stated period. A lease or rental provides use under its conditions and does not automatically give ownership.',
'For a small invented example with no other charges, $25 at pickup plus six payments of $15 totals $115. The first-date cash requirement and the whole-period total are different questions. Count a separately stated mandatory charge once and say whether taxes are included in the supplied amount.']],
['Compare the same period and what protection covers',[
'Use upfront amount + payment count × payment amount + additional mandatory charges = scheduled total. Compare offers over the same stated period. Keep possible future costs separate from confirmed scheduled costs.',
'A <strong>warranty</strong> or cover plan promises a remedy only under its terms. An exclusion identifies what is outside the cover; a claim fee can still be payable when a repair is eligible. “Covered” does not necessarily mean every future repair is free.',
'After calculating, check whether the payments fit the available money on their dates, what is owned at the end and which costs remain uncertain. A lower total can still require an upfront payment the person cannot meet.']]
], 'Read each offer for the amount due initially, number of payments and what each includes. The first check asks for the scheduled-total calculation. Later you will connect the payment comparison with ownership, cover limits and the supplied budget.');
add('fl1-05','Read a contract as a payment timeline',
'A phone or subscription offer may combine service, a device and a temporary promotional price. Learn to separate those parts before reading the offers.',[
['Distinguish service, device and commitment',[
'A <strong>subscription</strong> is a recurring arrangement for access to a service. A <strong>fixed term</strong> is a specified commitment period; month-to-month describes a shorter repeating period. Neither label supplies every cancellation condition. Read what ends, what continues and what remains payable.',
'A <strong>promotional price</strong> lasts for the period stated. A separate device payment can be due at the same time. For a simple example, $10 service plus a $5 device payment makes a $15 bill. If service later becomes $20 while the device payment continues, the bill becomes $25.']],
['Check the whole period and the hardest month',[
'Draw a timeline for the promotional months, later months and device-payment ending date. Multiply each combined monthly bill by the number of months it applies, then add separately due charges once.',
'A year’s total and an affordable first bill do not establish that later bills fit. Compare the largest required bill with the money available in that month. When examining an early exit, separate amounts already paid from the unpaid balance; ending service does not automatically erase a device obligation.',
'Use service price → device price → combined bill by period → total → later affordability → exit conditions. The course supplies the written terms for every required calculation.']]
], 'Read the price periods and device lines on both offers before the first check. That check asks for a combined bill, not the advertised service price alone. Later you will compare the full timeline and stated exit balance.');
add('fl1-06','Separate starting cash from ongoing cost',
'A move or purchase needs more than an advertised price. This lesson teaches how to identify money due at the start, costs that repeat, money potentially returned and amounts not yet known.',[
['Give each payment a category',[
'An <strong>upfront cost</strong> is due at the beginning. A <strong>recurring cost</strong> repeats over the use period. A <strong>refundable deposit</strong> is paid now and may be returned under stated conditions. It reduces cash available now even when a return is expected later.',
'For a simple example, $300 first rent and a $300 deposit require $600 initially. If the deposit is later fully returned, that return changes the lasting cost; it does not reduce the $600 needed on the first date. Count the first rent once when calculating a period that already includes it.']],
['Keep known and unknown costs visible',[
'An <strong>ownership cost</strong> or use cost helps keep an arrangement usable: for example, maintenance, utilities, storage or insurance where relevant. An <strong>estimate</strong> needs a basis and time period. A missing amount is unknown, not zero.',
'First calculate the known payments due by the start date and compare them with accessible money. Then calculate repeated commitments over the stated period, keeping deposit-return assumptions and irregular costs visible. A known minimum exceeding the budget already establishes a gap; a minimum that fits does not establish that every unknown will fit.',
'Use start-date cash → money remaining → recurring commitments → irregular or unknown costs → feasible change or specific question.']]
], 'Read the deposit condition before categorizing it. The first check asks how it affects move-in money. The later comparison uses both starting cash and the stated ownership or ongoing costs without inventing missing figures.');
add('fl2-01','Know what a borrowing offer’s numbers represent',
'A borrowing offer contains money received now and obligations paid later. Learn to distinguish those amounts before comparing the offers.',[
['Separate principal, charges and repayment',[
'<strong>Credit</strong> is an arrangement permitting borrowing. <strong>Debt</strong> is the amount owed. <strong>Principal</strong> is the amount borrowed before interest and other charges. <strong>Interest</strong> is a borrowing charge; a <strong>fee</strong> is another specified charge.',
'A <strong>payment</strong> is an amount due at a scheduled time. <strong>Total repayment</strong> includes returning the principal. <strong>Cost of borrowing</strong> is the amount paid above the principal, including the relevant fees in the comparison. Those measures answer different questions.']],
['Read the complete schedule',[
'For a small invented offer, receive $100, make six payments of $20 and pay a separate $5 fee. Total paid is $125; the amount above the $100 principal is $25. A $20 payment is neither the amount borrowed nor the full borrowing cost.',
'An <strong>instalment loan</strong> uses a stated payment series. A <strong>line of credit</strong> permits borrowing up to a limit under its terms; the amount actually borrowed may change. A limit is permission to borrow, not income. Read each product’s payment and access rules.',
'Use amount received → payment frequency and count → fees → total paid → amount above principal → fit with available repayment money. A longer series can produce a smaller payment while costing more overall.']]
], 'Identify which offer figure is a payment, principal, fee or total. The first check asks what one advertised payment describes. The later comparison asks you to calculate totals and explain whether the obligation fits the supplied budget.');
add('fl2-02','Use the same time unit as the interest rate',
'An interest rate needs an amount and a period before it gives a charge. This lesson uses a simple-interest model with an unchanged principal and the stated months-divided-by-12 rule.',[
['Read the formula’s quantities',[
'<strong>Principal</strong>, P, is the amount on which this model charges interest. The annual rate, r, must be a decimal: 6% means 6 ÷ 100 = 0.06. Time, t, must be in years when r is annual. <strong>Simple interest</strong> is I = P × r × t, without adding earlier interest to principal in this model.',
'Two months is 2 ÷ 12 year; six months is 0.5 year. Multiplying an annual rate by a number of months without conversion treats those months as years.']],
['Calculate the charge before the repayment',[
'In a separate invented example, $200 at 6% annually for two months gives $200 × 0.06 × 2/12 = $2 interest. With no fees, repayment is $202. The $2 charge and $202 repayment are different amounts.',
'Use principal → annual rate as a decimal → time in years → interest → principal plus interest and any separately stated fee. Label the result with dollars and its period. Round using the lesson’s stated convention.',
'This formula is for the specified unchanged-principal model. Reducing-balance payments and interest added to a balance require different calculations. The next loan-schedule lesson shows that distinction; do not assume every loan follows this formula for its full length.']]
], 'Read the agreement for the principal, annual rate, duration and time convention. Before the first check, convert the duration into the unit that belongs with the annual rate. The later tasks practise calculating and explaining the charge.');
add('fl2-03','Learn what each loan-payment column means',
'A loan payment can do two jobs: pay the interest charged for the period and reduce the original debt. Learn that split before reading the repayment schedule or using the calculator.',[
['Follow a payment through one row',[
'An <strong>amortization schedule</strong> records regular payments and the debt remaining after them. The <strong>opening balance</strong> is unpaid principal before this period’s charge and payment. <strong>Interest charged</strong> is the period’s borrowing charge. <strong>Principal repaid</strong> is the part of the payment reducing the debt. The <strong>closing balance</strong> is what remains afterwards.',
'For this lesson’s monthly model: opening balance × monthly rate = interest; payment − interest = principal repaid; opening balance − principal repaid = closing balance. That closing balance becomes the next row’s opening balance.']],
['Try a small example before the schedule',[
'In a separate invented row, opening debt is $200, the monthly rate is 1% and the payment is $22. Interest is $2, principal repaid is $20 and closing debt is $180. Subtracting the whole $22 from $200 would wrongly treat the interest portion as debt repayment.',
'The <strong>amortization period</strong> is the planned repayment length. Read the annual rate and the rule for converting it to a monthly rate before calculating. Alex’s and Ren’s supplied models divide the nominal annual rate by 12 and round money to cents.',
'Use the calculator to repeat a calculation you can explain: verify principal, rate, months and fee inputs, then compare payment size and total cost. A calculator result does not decide whether income or a purchase need will fit.']]
], 'Next, read Alex’s agreement and schedule. Locate the separate payment, interest and principal columns before the first check. The later explanation follows the complete case calculation, and the Tools calculator supports the repayment-length comparison.');
add('fl2-04','Understand minimum payments before choosing a strategy',
'A repayment strategy decides how to direct money among debts. It must start with what each account requires and how much repayment money is actually available.',[
['Protect required payments first',[
'A <strong>minimum payment</strong> is the least required by the account’s current terms. Extra money is what remains after all required minimums. Choosing a priority for extra payments does not remove the other account’s minimum.',
'For a small invented example, $70 is available and two minimums are $20 and $30. The minimums use $50, leaving $20 extra. If only $40 were available, there would instead be a $10 shortfall; no extra-payment strategy creates missing money.']],
['Compare the priority and its consequences',[
'<strong>Highest-rate-first</strong> directs extra money to the debt with the higher rate while maintaining other minimums. <strong>Smallest-balance-first</strong> directs it to the smaller debt, which may help motivation by clearing an account sooner. Compare the supplied schedules for total interest and finish date rather than assuming either always changes both.',
'A <strong>sustainable</strong> plan can be repeated while protecting necessary commitments. An emergency reserve has a separate purpose; do not count it as both reserved and spent on debt.',
'Use balances and rates → required minimums → available extra → stated strategy → schedule cost and timing → ability to continue. Hoped-for gambling winnings or uncertain investment gains are not confirmed repayment money.']]
], 'Read the statements for both minimums and the budget for the combined available amount. The first check asks how much is already committed to minimums. Only then compare where the remaining money goes.');
add('fl2-05','Decide whether credit meets a need on workable terms',
'Borrowing meets a payment now by committing money later. A useful decision begins with the need and deadline, then compares confirmed alternatives and the whole obligation.',[
['Name the function and timing',[
'A <strong>need</strong> is the function that must be met in the situation. Owning an item immediately may be one way to meet it; confirmed temporary access can be another. An <strong>alternative</strong> is usable only if its suitability, dates and costs are established.',
'For a simple example, a free, suitable loaner confirmed until a purchase deadline may allow time to save. A hoped-for loaner with no availability date cannot be treated the same way. Later income cannot pay an earlier bill unless a workable arrangement actually supplies access in time.']],
['Count future commitments as well as today’s access',[
'Add every payment and separate fee once. Compare that total with the cash price to identify extra financing cost. Check the payment dates against available money and the repayment length against the period of intended use.',
'<strong>Approval</strong> is a lender’s decision about an application; it is different from a borrower’s assessment of whether repayments fit. A <strong>co-borrower</strong> shares obligations under the agreement. That person’s consent and resources cannot be assumed.',
'Use need → deadline → confirmed alternatives → total and payment timing → recommendation with a condition. Explain a delay’s consequences as well as borrowing’s consequences. Inventing a new job, gift or refund would change the evidence.']]
], 'Read the access arrangement, saving dates and quotation together. The first check asks which confirmed fact makes an alternative possible. Later you will compare the financing cost and whether the payment sequence serves the course need.');
add('fl2-06','Separate the interest-rate rule from the payment rule',
'A fixed or variable rate describes how interest can change. It does not, by itself, explain what happens to the monthly payment. Read both rules before interpreting a loan comparison.',[
['Know what is fixed and what can change',[
'A <strong>fixed rate</strong> stays the same for the specified period. A <strong>variable rate</strong> can change under the agreement. A <strong>rate reset</strong> is the point where the model applies a new rate. Some agreements recalculate the payment; others can hold the payment while changing its split or repayment length.',
'The case’s written payment rule determines which behaviour to use. A recalculated payment uses the remaining balance and remaining repayment months, because earlier payments have already reduced the debt.']],
['Test the payment and the total separately',[
'A <strong>payment margin</strong> is available repayment money minus the required payment. With an invented $45 limit and $40 payment, the margin is $5. If a scenario raises the payment to $50, there is a $5 gap. A lower initial payment does not establish that later ones fit.',
'A <strong>stress test</strong> here means checking a less favourable supplied scenario. It is not a prediction of what rates will do or a lender’s approval procedure. Hold principal, payment frequency, repayment length and fees steady when comparing the rate paths.',
'Use rate rule → payment-change rule → supplied scenario outputs → margin in each period → total cost → recommendation. Keep a scenario’s possible outcome separate from a forecast or guarantee.']]
], 'Read what the variable agreement says happens when its rate changes. The first check asks about that payment behaviour. Later, compare the complete supplied rate paths and the budget margins rather than choosing from month one alone.');
add('fl2-07','A mortgage term and amortization are different clocks',
'A mortgage quotation can describe one contract period and a much longer planned repayment period. Learn that distinction before reading the quotation’s payment and balance figures.',[
['Read the contract and repayment plan separately',[
'The <strong>term</strong> is the period for which the current mortgage agreement applies. The <strong>amortization</strong> is the planned time to repay through scheduled payments under the model’s assumptions. A two-year term within a ten-year amortization would not mean the full loan is paid off after two years or the same rate is promised for ten.',
'The <strong>remaining balance</strong> is debt still owing at a stated date. At the end of a term, it must be addressed through repayment or an appropriate further agreement. A long-run calculator illustration keeping one rate is a conditional model.']],
['Look beyond the quoted payment',[
'A <strong>principal-and-interest payment</strong> covers those loan components. Housing can also involve taxes, insurance, utilities, maintenance and transaction costs. Check what a supplied budget limit already includes before using it.',
'A <strong>prepayment</strong> is principal paid beyond scheduled payments. A <strong>prepayment privilege</strong> states permitted amounts and timing without a specified charge. If it uses a percentage, identify the base: original principal, remaining balance and property value are different amounts.',
'Use borrowed money → contract-period payments → remaining debt at term end → costs outside the payment → flexibility conditions. A payment calculation alone does not establish loan approval or complete housing affordability.']]
], 'Locate the term and amortization separately in the quotation. The first check asks what ends at the stated term date. Later you will explain remaining debt and compare the supplied prepayment conditions without assuming an unchanged renewal rate.');
add('fl3-01','Give savings an amount, a purpose and a deadline',
'A savings goal turns “save more” into a plan you can check. Learn to calculate the remaining gap and compare all goals drawing on the same saving money.',[
['Find the gap and count the contributions',[
'A <strong>target</strong> is the required total. An <strong>opening amount</strong> is money already assigned to that goal. The <strong>gap</strong> is target minus that assigned amount. The <strong>time horizon</strong> is how long remains before the money is needed.',
'In a simple example, a $90 target with $30 already saved leaves $60. Three remaining saving dates require $20 on each for an even plan. Count contributions actually available before the deadline, not every date on the calendar.']],
['Check the shared limit and changing allocations',[
'A <strong>shared limit</strong> is the total available for all the goals together. If one needs $20 weekly and another $10, the combined requirement is $30. A $25 weekly budget cannot fund both even plans as written, although either calculation works by itself.',
'An <strong>allocation</strong> assigns part of the available money to a purpose. When one goal is funded, its contribution can move to another goal if the timeline permits. Carry forward money already saved rather than counting it twice.',
'Use purpose and date → gap → contributions → combined limit → checkpoint balances. Keep a separately protected emergency reserve separate. Short-term targets should not rely on an assumed investment gain arriving by the deadline.']]
], 'Read the goal board for the target, amount already assigned and saving dates. The first check asks for one remaining gap. Later, test whether the goals work together and redirect contributions after a goal is complete.');
add('fl3-02','Understand the different jobs money can do',
'The next available dollar can support a purchase, a reserve, debt repayment or investment. Learn what each use changes before comparing the supplied choices.',[
['Compare purpose, access and uncertainty',[
'<strong>Spending</strong> buys a good, service or experience. <strong>Saving</strong> sets money aside for a purpose. <strong>Debt repayment</strong> reduces an obligation. <strong>Investing</strong> places money in assets with possible gains and losses. The label alone does not establish suitability.',
'<strong>Liquidity</strong> concerns how readily value becomes available money, including time and cost. A reserve may need ready access. An extra debt payment may save future interest while leaving less accessible cash; whether it can be borrowed back depends on the actual terms.',
'Identify money available for this new choice after the commitments already protected. Do not reallocate rent or a reserve the case says must remain separate.']],
['Name the alternative you give up',[
'<strong>Opportunity cost</strong> is the value of the best feasible alternative given up when choosing. It is not the sum of every rejected option. If $20 can either build a reserve or reduce debt, choosing the reserve postpones that debt reduction. The same $20 cannot do both at once.',
'A <strong>return</strong> is a gain or loss relative to money invested, considering relevant costs. A pair of possible investment outcomes illustrates uncertainty; it does not establish their probabilities or promise the favourable one.',
'Use available money → purpose and deadline → access and risk → feasible alternatives → chosen use and opportunity cost. Explain the trade-off using the priorities actually supplied.']]
], 'Read the budget note to locate the amount being newly allocated, then the access and outcome records. The first check identifies which money is available. Later you will choose its purpose and explain the next-best alternative postponed.');
add('fl3-03','Separate the account from the investment inside it',
'An account arrangement and a holding describe different parts of an investment decision. Learn both layers before reading the options.',[
['Read the two layers',[
'An <strong>account arrangement</strong> supplies rules around eligible holdings. A <strong>holding</strong> is where the money is placed. A <strong>TFSA</strong> is an account arrangement with tax rules; a <strong>GIC</strong> is a holding with stated repayment and interest conditions. “A GIC held in a TFSA” names both layers.',
'An account name does not supply a holding’s rate, guarantee its value or make locked money accessible. The supplied case states the account eligibility and room needed here; no personal tax information is required.']],
['Compare the holding with the goal',[
'<strong>Liquidity</strong> describes how readily value becomes available money. <strong>Risk</strong> includes possible loss or failure to meet the goal. <strong>Return</strong> includes gains, losses and income, after relevant costs. A <strong>fee</strong> reduces what is kept. Read how and when money can be withdrawn or sold.',
'A holding saleable on a deadline may still have an uncertain sale value. A holding with a stated repayment date may not be accessible earlier. First identify when money is needed and what happens if it is worth less or unavailable.',
'Use goal and date → holding’s access, risk, return and fees → account rules → choice and remaining uncertainty. A willingness to take risk is different from having enough resources to bear a loss.']]
], 'Identify which record names an account arrangement and which names a holding before the first check. Later you will use the goal’s deadline and the supplied access terms to evaluate a combination of the two.');
add('fl3-04','A larger balance can buy less',
'The number of dollars and what those dollars can buy answer different questions. Learn the difference before reading the basket and savings records.',[
['Separate nominal money and purchasing power',[
'A <strong>nominal amount</strong> is the stated number of dollars. <strong>Purchasing power</strong> describes what money can buy at the relevant prices. <strong>Inflation</strong> is a rise in the general price level over time; an individual item’s quotation can change differently.',
'In a separate invented example, a fixed basket costs $100 initially and $104 later. A balance grows from $100 to $102. The person has two more dollars, but is $2 short of buying the same basket. No money needs to be withdrawn for purchasing power to decline.']],
['Keep the price and balance calculations separate',[
'A 4% price change uses a factor of 1.04: $100 × 1.04 = $104. To express the later $102 in that original price scale, divide by 1.04, giving about $98.08. This comparison amount is not a bank withdrawal or a new balance.',
'A <strong>fee</strong> is another cost. Its effect depends on what amount it applies to and when. When comparing model runs, hold the growth and price assumptions steady if the purpose is to isolate the fee.',
'Use the goal’s price → available nominal balance → gap → price-adjusted comparison if requested. A calculator’s chosen growth or inflation rate is a scenario input, not a forecast. The long-term compounding investigation remains optional and separate from the required purchasing-power task.']]
], 'Read the two records as the same basket and the same year. Before the first check, compare what is in the account with the basket’s new price. Later you will inspect the fee model and update a plan using a specific confirmed quotation.');
add('fl3-05','Make several goals share one workable plan',
'A financial plan connects purposes, amounts, dates and actions. Learn to check several goals against one contribution limit before reading the goal records.',[
['Calculate each gap, then combine the contributions',[
'A <strong>goal</strong> states what the money is for, how much is needed and when. The <strong>gap</strong> is target minus money already assigned. A <strong>contribution</strong> is a planned addition. A combined monthly limit applies to all goals together, not separately to each.',
'In a simple example, a $100 target with $40 assigned leaves $60. Over three monthly saving dates that requires $20 monthly. If another goal needs $15 monthly, the combined $35 does not fit a $30 limit without an adjustment.']],
['Use phases and review points',[
'A <strong>phase</strong> is a period with the same allocation rule. When an earlier goal is funded, its contribution can move to a later goal if the dates permit. At each boundary, carry the existing balance forward; do not reuse opening money as a new contribution.',
'An <strong>action</strong> says what moves, where and when. A <strong>review point</strong> says when to check prices, available money, dates and priorities. “Save more” supplies neither.',
'Use goals and gaps → available saving dates → combined limit → phased allocations → checkpoint balances → review. Choose planning support for a specific question: a calculator can check a projection, while a provider record can confirm an entry condition. Neither guarantees a personal outcome.']]
], 'Read the monthly saving note before calculating either goal. The first check identifies the maximum combined contribution. Later you will show how contributions change after one goal is funded and identify a useful review trigger.');
add('fl3-06','Revise from the money and time remaining now',
'A plan needs revision when actual events change its assumptions. Learn to separate what has already happened from what remains possible before reading the changed savings record.',[
['Start from the current balance',[
'A <strong>transaction</strong> is a movement such as a deposit or payment. The <strong>current balance</strong> reflects transactions already made. A <strong>plan variance</strong> is a difference between the plan and what actually happened. A <strong>revised projection</strong> starts from the new facts rather than rewriting earlier ones.',
'For a simple example, after an expense the current balance is $90, the target is $150 and two deposits remain. The gap is $60, so an even plan requires $30 per remaining deposit. An old opening balance or elapsed contribution date cannot be reused as though the expense and time had not occurred.']],
['Change a condition openly',[
'A revision can change the contribution, target, deadline or a confirmed resource. A higher contribution must fit the budget; a reduced target must still meet the need; a later deadline must actually be flexible. A hoped-for gift or price recovery is not a confirmed resource.',
'Keep a one-time cost separate from ongoing costs. Name which condition changes and why, then state a <strong>review trigger</strong>: an event that would require another check.',
'Use actual event → present balance → remaining gap and saving dates → feasible change → review. These required saving cases assume no interest. Any optional growth investigation is a separate model and does not supply extra money for them.']]
], 'Read the actual contributions and expense before the revised budget. The first check asks for the balance immediately after the event. Later you will count only the remaining saving dates and explain an adjustment that fits the supplied limits.');
add('fl4-01','Understand what an online detail can reveal',
'A digital footprint is information left through online activity. Learn to connect a visible clue with a plausible exposure and a suitable control before reading the supplied posts and settings.',[
['Separate a clue from a proven conclusion',[
'A <strong>routine clue</strong> is a detail that may suggest a repeated time or place. A named venue and repeated departure time together can reveal more than either alone. An <strong>inference</strong> is a possible interpretation; it is not proof of an exact address, harmful intent or a completed attack.',
'For a simple example, “Practice at the same hall every Thursday at 5” may let a viewer infer a regular visit. “I enjoyed practice” shared later with a neutral background can serve a similar social purpose while revealing less of the routine.']],
['Choose controls for different exposures',[
'<strong>Data minimization</strong> means sharing only the information needed for the purpose. An audience setting affects who sees a post; app permissions affect what an app can access. A unique password reduces the effect of password reuse; <strong>multi-factor authentication</strong> adds a verification step to sign-in.',
'These controls have different jobs. A privacy setting does not replace securing an exposed account, and a stronger password does not remove a public schedule. Read which exposures the record actually supplies.',
'Use exact detail → plausible inference and limit → relevant control → remaining purpose → review habit. Knowledge of someone’s team or routine does not authenticate a message asking for payment.']]
], 'Read the post, audience and account information separately. The first check asks which details together suggest a routine. Later you will match distinct changes to public exposure and account access without inventing an incident.');
add('fl4-02','Judge a message by its requested action',
'A convincing message may use a familiar name, logo or story. Learn to identify the action it requests and verify through an independent route before classifying the supplied messages.',[
['Locate the action and possible loss',[
'A <strong>scam</strong> uses deception to obtain a benefit. An <strong>indicator</strong> is a clue worth checking, not proof by itself. Read the verb: send money, reveal a code, install an app, approve access or transfer funds. Then identify what could be lost: money, information, account access or device control.',
'<strong>Pressure</strong> can use urgency, a threat, an easy-return promise or concern for another person. Good spelling and a real organization’s name do not establish that the sender represents it.']],
['Break the sender’s control of the check',[
'<strong>Independent verification</strong> uses a trusted route obtained separately from the message. A link or telephone number supplied by the suspicious sender may return you to the same deception. Use an already known contact or an official service reached separately.',
'For a simple example, a message saying “send your sign-in code to keep your account” asks for access information. Pause the interaction and reach the service independently; knowing your name does not verify the request.',
'Use requested action → exposure → pressure or other clues → separate verification route → confirmed or unresolved status. A category such as job, investment, purchase or family-emergency fraud explains a pattern; the actual request supplies the evidence.']]
], 'Read the action requested in each card before judging its appearance. The first check asks which request creates an immediate money or access risk. Later you will classify the pattern and choose a specific independent verification route.');
add('fl4-03','Map what was exposed before choosing a response',
'When information may have been disclosed, a useful response starts with the confirmed exposure and the connections between services. Learn that distinction before reading the incident record.',[
['Separate information entered from harm confirmed',[
'An <strong>exposure</strong> means information or access may have left the person’s control. A <strong>confirmed event</strong> is something the record establishes. Entering a password on a suspicious page supports action to secure affected accounts; it does not by itself establish a stolen identity document or money transfer.',
'A <strong>recovery route</strong> is the genuine provider’s process for protecting or regaining access. An <strong>account connection</strong> exists when one service can reset or recover another. Reusing an exposed password can affect more than the first service.']],
['Match controls to the connections',[
'Stop the suspicious interaction and use an independently reached trusted provider route. Identify affected accounts and recovery links, then prioritize using those facts. In a simple model where Account A resets Account B, leaving A exposed can undermine a change to B.',
'A password change, ending signed-in sessions, checking recovery entries and enabling MFA address different access routes. Follow the supplied service rules; a password change does not necessarily end an existing session.',
'Use confirmed exposure → affected services and connections → timely protective actions → what was done → unresolved checks. A report can name categories of information without copying actual passwords, codes or identification numbers into coursework.']]
], 'Read what the person entered and which services share the credential. The first check asks what exposure is established. Later you will explain a protective sequence and distinguish completed actions from results still needing confirmation.');
add('fl4-04','Compare the advertised promise with the written terms',
'A consumer concern becomes clearer when the advertisement, price and accepted terms are compared on the same basis. Learn to label those records before reading the supplied offer.',[
['Hold the purchase details steady',[
'A <strong>mandatory fee</strong> must be paid to obtain the stated offer. An <strong>optional charge</strong> can be declined. Compare the same item, quantity and choices at each stage, and keep before-tax and after-tax prices separate.',
'For a simple invented offer, an advertisement says $40 before tax and checkout says $45 before tax for the unchanged item. The $5 difference is separate from tax. If it comes from a charge the customer cannot decline, name that required charge when explaining the concern.',
'A <strong>subscription term</strong> states the commitment and conditions. A monthly payment describes payment frequency; it does not alone establish that the agreement ends every month or can be cancelled without further obligations.']],
['Write a concern that can be investigated',[
'Use promise → conflicting record → effect → specific requested response. Identify dates, amounts and relevant wording. Preserve the available originals rather than replacing an earlier advertisement with a corrected one.',
'A <strong>merchant dispute</strong> concerns a recognized purchase or its terms. A transaction not made or approved by the person is a different fact pattern. Describe which occurred instead of treating every unwanted charge as unauthorized.',
'Choose a route for the job it can do: explaining or correcting the offer, addressing a payment concern, or reviewing a broader consumer issue. A request, acknowledgement and confirmed correction are separate statuses.']]
], 'Read the advertisement and checkout for the same purchase and tax basis. The first check asks which fact supports the price concern. Later you will document the conflict, cancellation conditions where supplied and a suitable response route.');
add('fl4-05','Coordinate help around the actual exposure',
'A recovery plan can involve several services, but each should have a reason to act. Learn to map the confirmed loss or information exposure to the provider that can address it.',[
['Make an exposure map',[
'A <strong>recovery plan</strong> connects risks with actions and follow-up. Identify categories actually involved: a payment, account credential, identity information or device access. Then identify the provider or official route with responsibility for that category.',
'For a simple example, a deceptive transfer and an exposed email password create a financial branch and an account-security branch. Reporting a payment does not change the password, and changing a password does not recover the payment. Additional agencies need a relevant reason, not merely the fact that an incident occurred.']],
['Keep actions and outcomes distinct',[
'A <strong>status log</strong> separates action planned, action taken with a time or reference, and confirmed result or next check. “Reported” does not mean “refunded.” “Password changed” does not mean “all other sessions ended.” Explain what is still unresolved.',
'Use confirmed exposure → time-sensitive protection → provider-specific requests → reporting where relevant → follow-up. Do not delay urgent contact to perfect a record. Use independently reached routes, not contact details supplied by the suspicious sender.',
'A <strong>recovery scam</strong> offers another deceptive rescue, often requesting a fee, code or secrecy. A promised recovery is not evidence of authority or success. Keep existing evidence safely and do not send another payment or code to prove entitlement to help.']]
], 'Read the payment and credential events separately before matching routes. The first check asks which response fits a confirmed financial event. Later you will coordinate the account, reporting and follow-up actions and resist a second-loss request.');
